-- Realign reports.nsfw with the source catalogue.
--
-- The catalogue owns this flag for any report against a source that exists in
-- it: it comes from the upstream extension index, which is the same signal the
-- apps use to hide a source. The original import in scripts/import-issues.ts
-- took it from a GitHub `18+` label instead, and nobody maintains those, so
-- 46 of the 174 catalogue-backed rows disagreed with the catalogue and 45
-- reports about adult sources sat on the default board. hanime.tv was the one
-- that surfaced it: its issue carried no label, so report 126 read as ordinary.
--
-- Nothing in the running system could correct them, because a sync pass only
-- ever wrote `status` to a linked report. That is fixed too — reconcileNsfw in
-- src/lib/github-sync.ts re-derives the flag on every pass, which is what keeps
-- this correct from here on and what makes an upstream flag change propagate.
-- This migration is the one-time repair of the rows that predate it.
--
-- The two id lists are a snapshot of src/data/sources.json as it stood when
-- this was written (108 marked 18+, 202 not, 310 total). That is
-- what a migration is for; it is not the maintenance mechanism and does not
-- need to stay current. Same reasoning as the CASE in 0005, which froze a copy
-- of problemKeyFor.
--
-- Rows with no source_id are deliberately untouched. A source request has no
-- catalogue entry, so its flag is the filer's own declaration and a moderator's
-- to correct, and no automatic rule may overwrite that.
UPDATE `reports` SET `nsfw` = 1, `updated_at` = unixepoch()
